          Water-based (latex) wood filler in a shallow nail hole or scratch is usually dry enough to sand in 15 minutes to 2 hours, while a deep fill can take several hours to overnight and should be built up in layers rather than packed in at once. Two-part fillers do not dry at all; they cure chemically, with polyester types sandable in well under an hour and epoxy types typically giving 15 to 30 minutes of working time and needing a few hours before sanding. Depth and temperature move every one of those numbers, and the label on your tub is the final word. Below are the timings by type, what slows them down, and how to test a patch before you reach for the sandpaper.
        </p>

        <div id="by-type" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Why Does Drying Time Vary So Much Between Fillers?
          </h2>

          <p>
            The phrase wood filler covers products that harden in completely different ways. Water-based fillers, often sold as latex or acrylic fillers, harden as the water evaporates out of them. Solvent-based fillers harden the same way, except the solvent is something like acetone, which leaves much faster. Two-part fillers, whether polyester or epoxy, harden through a chemical reaction between a base and a hardener and do not need air at all.
          </p>

          <p>
            That difference changes how depth affects the wait. Evaporating fillers dry from the outside in, so a deep fill forms a hard skin while the core stays soft, and the time grows much faster than the depth. Two-part fillers cure through their whole thickness at roughly the same rate, so a deep repair is ready at about the same time as a shallow one, and in fact larger masses can cure faster because the reaction gives off heat.
          </p>

          <p>
            It is also worth separating wood filler from wood putty, because people use the words interchangeably. Wood filler hardens and is meant to be sanded before finishing. Traditional wood putty, often oil-based and sold in coloured sticks or tubs, is meant to be pressed into nail holes after the finish is applied and stays slightly pliable. It never dries hard enough to sand, which is a feature, not a defect. Our explainer on <a href="/spackle-vs-putty/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">spackle vs wood putty vs wood filler</a> sets out which to use where.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
