            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Treat those as typical ranges at room temperature, not promises. Formulations vary even within a category, and some water-based fillers are marketed as fast-drying while others are designed to stay workable longer. Read the tub, then use the tests later on this page to confirm the patch is actually ready.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="water-based" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. How Long Does Water-Based Wood Filler Take to Dry?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Water-based filler is what most people buy for interior trim, furniture touch-ups and nail holes, and it is the easiest to live with: low odour, soap and water cleanup, and fairly quick drying. A thin skim over a scratch or a small nail hole is often sandable in 15 to 30 minutes in a warm room. A typical larger nail hole or shallow ding is usually ready within one to two hours.
          </p>

          <p>
            Deeper fills are where people get caught out. Many water-based fillers recommend applying no more than about a quarter inch at a time. Pack a half-inch gouge in one go and the outside will look dry in an hour while the inside stays soft for many hours, sometimes overnight or longer. Sand it early and you will tear soft filler out of the hole. Fill it in two or three layers instead, letting each one dry through, and the total time is usually shorter.
          </p>

          <p>
            Water-based filler also shrinks as the water leaves. A fill that looked flush when wet often dries slightly dished. The fix is to overfill a little, leaving a small crown above the surface that you sand flush once dry. On deeper holes, the shrinkage on the first layer is more pronounced, which is another reason layering beats one big fill.
          </p>

          <p>
            Many water-based fillers change colour as they dry, often lightening by a shade or two. That is a handy visual cue, but it reflects the surface, not the core. On anything deeper than a nail hole, treat the colour change as the first sign rather than the final answer. Our <a href="/best-wood-filler/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">best wood filler guide</a> compares the water-based, solvent-based and two-part options we recommend for trim, furniture and exterior repairs.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="solvent-based" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. How Long Does Solvent-Based Wood Filler Take to Dry?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Solvent-based fillers are the traditional hardware-store wood dough products, made with wood flour and binders in a solvent such as acetone. Because the solvent flashes off fast, shallow fills often harden in 15 minutes to an hour. That speed is the main appeal. The downsides are a strong smell, flammable vapour that needs good ventilation, and a short working time before the filler in the tub starts to stiffen.
          </p>

          <p>
            Deeper solvent-based fills slow down for the same reason water-based ones do: the outside hardens and slows the escape of solvent from the middle. Expect several hours for a deeper fill, and layer anything substantial. Solvent-based fillers can shrink noticeably as the solvent leaves, so overfill each layer slightly and expect to sand back to flush.
          </p>

          <p>
            Once hard, solvent-based filler is tough and sands well, and some versions are rated for exterior use, which is part of why it remains popular for outdoor trim and furniture. Keep the lid tight between scoops and work quickly. If the filler in the tub thickens, many products can be revived with a little of the solvent named on the label, but do not thin it so far that it shrinks even more in the hole.
          </p>

          <p>
            If the smell or flammability is a problem for an indoor job, a good water-based filler or a two-part epoxy will usually do the same work. Save solvent-based filler for jobs where its hardness and speed actually matter, and always follow the ventilation and storage instructions on the tin.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="two-part" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. How Long Do Two-Part and Epoxy Wood Fillers Take?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Two-part fillers are the answer for deep holes, rotted sections, exterior repairs and anything structural. Because they cure chemically, they do not shrink in the way evaporating fillers do, and depth matters far less. The clock starts the moment you mix the two parts, and it is the working time, not the drying time, that usually catches beginners out.
          </p>

          <p>
            Polyester-based two-part fillers, which use a cream hardener mixed into a resin filler, are fast. Working time is often only a few minutes once mixed, and the repair can typically be shaped or sanded within roughly 15 to 30 minutes at room temperature. They are excellent for quick, hard repairs, but you need to mix small batches, because a large batch can harden on the mixing board before you have used it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Epoxy wood fillers, often sold as two putties mixed in equal parts, are slower and more forgiving. Typical working time is around 15 to 30 minutes, the repair can usually be sanded after a few hours, and full cure is often quoted at around 24 hours. Epoxy bonds very well to sound wood, holds up to weather once painted, and is the usual choice for repairing rotted window sills and door frames after the soft wood has been cut out.
          </p>

          <p>
            Mix ratio and temperature control the whole process. Too little hardener, or two parts mixed unevenly, can leave soft spots that never cure, and too much can make polyester fillers brittle. Cold slows the reaction dramatically; many epoxies cure poorly below around 50 to 60°F, and the label will give a minimum. Warmth speeds it up and shortens working time. Read the specific product's ratios and times, because they vary widely.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Evaporating fillers: layer deep holes. Two-part fillers: mix small batches.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Water- and solvent-based fillers punish depth. Two-part fillers punish slow hands. Choose by the repair, then work to the filler's limits.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="depth-temp" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. How Do Depth, Temperature and Humidity Change the Wait?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Depth is the single biggest variable for water-based and solvent-based fillers. A skim coat a few hundredths of an inch thick has a tiny amount of water or solvent to lose and a lot of surface to lose it from. A deep fill has much more to lose through the same small surface, and once a skin forms on top, it slows everything below. That is why doubling the depth can far more than double the wait.
          </p>

          <p>
            Temperature affects every type. Warm rooms speed evaporation and chemical curing; cold rooms slow both. Most fillers are designed for use somewhere around 50 to 90°F, and below the minimum on the label, water-based filler may stay soft for a long time and two-part fillers may never reach full hardness. If you are working in a cold garage or on exterior trim in autumn, warm the area or wait for a better day.
          </p>

          <p>
            Humidity mainly matters for water-based filler, which dries by losing water to the air. In a damp basement or on a muggy day, expect longer times than the label suggests. A dehumidifier or gentle airflow across the room helps. A hair dryer or heat gun held close to the patch is a poor shortcut, because it skins the surface while the core stays wet, which can lead to cracking or shrinkage after you have sanded and finished.
          </p>

          <p>
            The wood itself matters too. Filler in very dry, porous wood loses water into the wood as well as the air and can dry faster. Filler in damp wood dries slowly and may not bond well. Exterior wood that has been wet should dry out before you fill it, both for drying time and because sealing moisture into a repair invites rot.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-to-test" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. How Can You Tell When Wood Filler Is Dry Enough to Sand?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Start with colour, then move to touch. Many water-based fillers dry a shade lighter than they go on, so an even colour across the whole patch, with no darker centre, is the first good sign. A darker spot in the middle of a larger fill usually means the core is still wet. Two-part fillers do not change colour as they cure, so you need the touch tests for those.
          </p>

          <p>
            Press a fingernail into the edge of the patch where a mark will not matter. If it dents or feels rubbery, it is not ready. If the nail leaves no impression and the filler feels as hard as the surrounding wood, sanding should be fine. For water-based filler, also touch the patch with the back of a finger: a patch that feels cool compared with the wood around it is still evaporating water.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then do a test sand. Use fine paper, around 120 to 180 grit for shaping and finer for finishing, and take a couple of light strokes. Dry filler turns to fine powder and sands flush with the wood. Filler that is still soft clogs the paper, smears or pulls out of the hole. If that happens, stop and give it more time; sanding harder only makes a bigger hole to refill.
          </p>

          <p>
            When filling with a two-part product, keep a small blob of the mixed batch on your mixing board. Because it is the same mix in the same room, it cures at the same rate as the repair. When the blob is rock hard and sands to powder, the repair is too, and you have not had to test on the work itself. For flat areas, a <a href="/best-palm-sander/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">palm sander</a> speeds up levelling, but finish edges by hand so you do not dish the surrounding wood.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Typical Sand-Ready Times</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">15-120 min</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Water-based, shallow</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">Overnight</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Water-based, deep in one pass</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">15-30 min</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Polyester two-part</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">A few hours</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Epoxy two-part</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="stain-paint" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Can You Stain or Paint Wood Filler Once It's Dry?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Painting is easy. Once the filler is dry and sanded flush, prime it along with the rest of the bare wood and paint as usual. Filler is usually more or less absorbent than the surrounding wood, so skipping the primer can leave a patch that shows as a dull or shiny spot through the paint. A quick spot of primer solves it. If you are prepping a whole room, our <a href="/best-primer/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">best primer guide</a> covers options for trim and doors.
          </p>

          <p>
            Staining is harder, and this is where honest advice differs from tub marketing. Many fillers are labelled stainable, but wood filler does not have grain or pores like real wood, so it rarely absorbs stain the way the surrounding wood does. Patches often come out lighter or darker, and flat-looking. Test your filler and stain together on a scrap of the same wood before committing to the real piece.
          </p>

          <p>
            For stained work there are better approaches. Choose a pre-tinted filler close to the final stained colour rather than the raw wood colour, or tint a neutral filler with a little of the stain or a universal tint before applying. For small nail holes in finished trim, it is often easier to stain and finish the wood first, then fill holes with a matching coloured wood putty afterward, which blends better and never needs sanding.
          </p>

          <p>
            Before any clear finish goes over a filled patch, make sure the filler is dry through, not just on top. A soft core under polyurethane can leave a dimple or a cloudy spot. If you are finishing a floor or tabletop, our sibling guide on <a href="/how-long-does-polyurethane-take-to-dry/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">how long polyurethane takes to dry</a> explains recoat and cure timing so the filler and finish both get the time they need. Patching walls instead of wood is a different job; see <a href="/how-long-does-spackle-take-to-dry/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">how long spackle takes to dry</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Filling a deep hole in one pass</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Water-based and solvent-based fillers dry from the outside in. A thick fill looks dry on top within an hour but stays soft underneath for hours or overnight, then shrinks and cracks. Build deep holes in layers of about a quarter inch, letting each dry through, or switch to a two-part filler that cures throughout.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sanding as soon as the surface looks dry</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A colour change on top only tells you about the surface. Sanding a soft core drags filler out of the hole and leaves a crater bigger than the original. Check with a fingernail and a light test sand first, and if the paper clogs or the filler smears, stop and wait longer.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Mixing a big batch of two-part filler</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Polyester fillers can start to harden within minutes, and even slower epoxies only give 15 to 30 minutes of working time. Mix more than you can apply and the rest goes hard on the board. Mix small batches, measure the hardener accurately, and combine the parts thoroughly to avoid soft spots.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting stainable on the label</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Filler has no grain, so it rarely absorbs stain like the surrounding wood and often ends up a different shade. Test filler and stain together on scrap wood first. For stained work, use a pre-tinted filler matched to the finished colour, or fill nail holes with coloured putty after finishing.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Filling in a cold room or on wet wood</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Below the label's minimum temperature, water-based filler may stay soft and two-part filler may never fully cure. Damp wood slows drying and weakens the bond, and sealing moisture in can encourage rot outdoors. Warm the space, let exterior wood dry out, and check the temperature range before you start.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does wood filler take to dry before painting?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Paint only once the filler is hard all the way through and sanded flush. For water-based filler in small holes that is often within one to two hours, but deep fills may need overnight. Two-part epoxy usually needs a few hours before sanding and priming. Prime the patch before painting so it does not show as a dull spot.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you speed up wood filler drying?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, within reason. Warm the room to a comfortable temperature, lower humidity with a dehumidifier, and keep gentle air moving across the room. Fill deep holes in thin layers rather than one thick fill. Avoid aiming a heat gun or hair dryer at the patch, which hardens the surface and traps moisture underneath.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why is my wood filler cracking as it dries?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Cracking usually means the fill was too deep for one pass, so the outside shrank as it dried around a soft core, or it was dried too fast with direct heat. It can also happen if the filler was old and partly dried in the tub. Dig out the cracked filler, then refill in thinner layers.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is wood filler waterproof once dry?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most interior water-based fillers are not; they can soften with prolonged moisture and should be sealed with paint or finish. Some fillers are rated for exterior use, and two-part epoxy fillers are highly water-resistant once cured. For outdoor trim, sills or anything that gets wet, use an exterior-rated or epoxy filler and protect it with paint.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does wood filler last in the tub once opened?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on how well the lid seals. Water-based and solvent-based fillers gradually dry out from exposure to air, often forming a crust on top. Press plastic wrap onto the surface before closing the lid and store at room temperature. If the filler has become crumbly or stiff, replace it rather than using it.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you put wood filler over old wood filler?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, if the old filler is sound, hard and well bonded. Sand it lightly, clean off dust and apply the new layer. If the old filler is cracked, loose or crumbling, dig it out first, because new filler over a failing patch will fail with it. With two-part fillers, lightly scuff the cured surface before adding more.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between drying and curing for wood filler?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Water- and solvent-based fillers dry as the liquid evaporates, and once hard through they are effectively done. Two-part fillers cure through a chemical reaction; they can be sanded once hard enough but continue gaining strength, often for 24 hours. For heavy use or exterior exposure, give two-part repairs their full cure time before finishing.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

