        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Water-based wood filler in a shallow hole is usually ready to sand within 15 minutes to two hours; deep fills take much longer and should be built in layers. Solvent-based filler is a little faster on the surface, polyester two-part filler is fastest of all, and epoxy gives you more working time and a few hours before sanding. Depth and temperature move every one of those numbers.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Whatever you use, test before you sand: even colour, a fingernail that leaves no mark, and sandpaper that produces dust rather than smears. Give the patch the time it needs, prime it before painting, and test any stain on scrap first. A few minutes of patience is what makes a repair disappear.
          </p>
